RustSec advisory-db snapshot
============================

This directory is a verbatim export of the RustSec advisory database at a pinned commit, vendored
so that the `security` workflow's cargo-audit gate needs no network access (T-CI-002).

  repository: https://github.com/RustSec/advisory-db.git
  commit:     074ea9a7ed619f511b5b7374c7a97e98d1e98be4
  commit date: 2026-11-19 17:35:07 +0100
  subject:    wasmtime: mark versions before the affected ranges as unaffected
  exported:   git archive <commit>  (no .git directory: a plain tree is what cargo-audit --db reads)
  files:      1569
  advisories: 1550

Do NOT edit files here by hand. Refresh with:

  git -C ~/.cargo/advisory-db fetch
  git -C ~/.cargo/advisory-db checkout <new-commit>
  rm -rf caddy-rs/.cargo/advisory-db
  git -C ~/.cargo/advisory-db archive HEAD | tar -x -C caddy-rs/.cargo/advisory-db
  # then update commit/date/subject/files/advisories above, and re-run the gate below

Why vendored rather than cloned at run time: a run-time `git clone` makes the verdict depend on
whatever the upstream repository holds that day, so a RustSec change can turn the gate red (or
green) without any change in this repository. Pinning the database makes the audit reproducible
and reviewable: bumping it is a commit, and the diff shows which advisories arrived.

Refresh cadence: at least every release (see docs/operations/RELEASE-READINESS.md in the program
repository). A stale database is a known, recorded limitation, not a silent one -- the commit
above names exactly how stale the snapshot is.
